  "youtubeUrl": "https://youtu.be/q2_BpJPnU_g",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "On turn 3 with 3 lands available (Spirebluff Canal, Riverpyre Verge, and the Verge can tap for U if controlling an Island or Mountain - Spirebluff Canal counts as neither type, so Riverpyre Verge only taps for R), the player has Duelist of the Mind (1U) which is a key tempo creature in this deck. With Spirebluff Canal providing U and Riverpyre Verge providing R, the player actually has 1R+1U available, which is enough to cast Duelist of the Mind (1U, paying 1 generic with R). Deploying Duelist on turn 3 adds a flying vigilance threat that synergizes with the draw-heavy gameplan and Stormchaser's Talent. Passing without casting anything wastes mana and tempo.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed priority without casting any spell, leaving mana unused.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Duelist of the Mind for 1U (using Spirebluff Canal for U and Riverpyre Verge for R as the generic mana), deploying a flying vigilance creature that grows with card draw and pairs well with Frostcliff Siege on the following turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 22,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 202
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The opponent has no creatures on the battlefield (their graveyard contains lands and a sorcery, and no battlefield creatures are listed). The Otter Token is a 1/1 with prowess that could attack freely for 1 damage with zero risk. There's no reason to hold back a free attack into an empty board.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to attack with the Otter Token.",
      "betterLine": "Attack with the Otter Token for 1 free damage against an empty board.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 30,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 272
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Duelist of the Mind in the precombat main phase is suboptimal. Duelist's power equals the number of cards drawn this turn, which is currently 0 (assuming the draw for turn already happened, it would be 1). Playing it pre-combat means it will likely attack as a 1/3 or 0/3. More importantly, casting a creature before combat when holding tricks like Into the Flood Maw and Torch the Tower is generally worse than attacking first with the Otter token (which has prowess and could benefit from post-combat spells). Additionally, Fear of Missing Out could have been a stronger play here — it provides a 2/3 body, triggers a loot on ETB which could help fuel delirium, and with delirium active it grants extra combat phases. Frostcliff Siege (choosing Jeskai) would also have been worth considering as it draws cards on combat damage, synergizing with the Otter already on board.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Duelist of the Mind precombat on turn 3 with 3 lands available.",
      "betterLine": "Attack with Otter first, then in second main phase consider casting Fear of Missing Out (which loots on ETB and can enable delirium/extra combats) or Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai mode for card draw on combat damage). If casting Duelist, do it post-combat to preserve information and combat trick flexibility.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 45,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 513
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The opponent has no creatures on the battlefield and no untapped lands visible to threaten interaction. Attacking with the 1/1 Otter Token is free damage with zero risk — there's nothing to block it. The Otter is a 1/1 prowess token that could also benefit from spells cast in second main. Additionally, with Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai mode) potentially coming down later, establishing a pattern of connecting with combat damage matters. Missing 1 damage repeatedly adds up, and there's no downside to attacking here against an empty board.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to attack with the Otter Token, holding back all creatures.",
      "betterLine": "Attack with the Otter Token for 1 free damage against the empty board. There are no blockers and no reason to hold it back.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 49,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 529
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Playing Tiger-Seal in the precombat main phase is debatable. Tiger-Seal taps itself at the beginning of your upkeep and only untaps when you draw your second card each turn. With Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai mode) on the battlefield, combat damage draws a card, but Tiger-Seal won't be able to attack this turn anyway since it's a new creature without haste. More importantly, casting Tiger-Seal before combat means the Otter Token and Duelist of the Mind won't benefit from prowess triggers during combat this turn. If instead the player attacked first with the Otter and Duelist (Duelist has 0 power since no cards drawn yet this turn, but Otter could attack), then cast Tiger-Seal post-combat, the sequencing would be slightly better. Additionally, casting Duelist of the Mind #2 might have been considered as an alternative play, though Tiger-Seal is a reasonable card to deploy. The main issue is casting a creature before combat when there's no urgency, potentially missing prowess value from combat tricks.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Tiger-Seal in precombat main phase",
      "betterLine": "Attack first with Otter Token (potentially triggering Frostcliff Siege's Jeskai draw on combat damage), then cast Tiger-Seal in post-combat main to preserve the option of casting instants like Into the Flood Maw or Torch the Tower before/during combat for prowess triggers on the Otter.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 74,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 827
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast Duelist of the Mind with only 2 untapped lands, leaving 0 mana open, when they had Multiversal Passage in hand to play first. Playing the land first would leave 1 mana open to hold up Into the Flood Maw or Torch the Tower as interaction.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Duelist of the Mind without first playing Multiversal Passage from hand",
      "betterLine": "Play Multiversal Passage first (choosing Island), then cast Duelist of the Mind for {1}{U}, leaving 1 mana open for Torch the Tower or Into the Flood Maw",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 81,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 902
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Declined to attack into a completely empty board. The opponent has no creatures to block. Even without pre-combat spell casting, attacking with Fear of Missing Out (2/3), Otter Token (1/1), and any Duelists with non-zero power deals free damage and triggers Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai) to draw a card on combat damage. Skipping combat entirely wastes significant damage and card advantage.",
      "actionTaken": "Skipped combat entirely, choosing not to attack with any creatures, reasoning that spells needed to be cast first for prowess triggers.",
      "betterLine": "Attack with all creatures into the empty board. Even without pre-combat spells, Fear of Missing Out deals 2, Otter deals 1, and Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai) draws a card on combat damage. Could cast spells pre-combat to further pump prowess/Duelist, but declining to attack at all is strictly worse than attacking.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 102,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 1180
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast Boomerang Basics in postcombat main phase before playing Riverpyre Verge from hand. The reasoning about lethal is completely wrong since combat already happened - no prowess triggers or power boosts matter this turn. Should have played land first to maximize available mana for additional spells this turn.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Boomerang Basics postcombat before playing their land drop (Riverpyre Verge still in hand)",
      "betterLine": "Play Riverpyre Verge first to have maximum mana available, then cast Boomerang Basics and potentially follow up with additional spells like Torch the Tower with the extra mana.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 103,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 1180
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarding Into the Flood Maw (a 1-mana interactive spell that triggers prowess and can bounce future threats) when holding two redundant lands (Riverpyre Verge and Spirebluff Canal) with already 5+ lands on board. The cheap spell has more utility than extra land drops at this stage of the game.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Into the Flood Maw as one of the two discards",
      "betterLine": "Discard Riverpyre Verge and Spirebluff Canal (or the second Frostcliff Siege) as the two discards, keeping Into the Flood Maw for its cheap interaction and prowess triggering",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 125,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 1419
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarding Quantum Riddler over a redundant land when Quantum Riddler has Warp {1}{U} (very cheap deployment), draws a card on entry (synergizing with Frostcliff Siege Jeskai and Duelist of the Mind), and provides a 4/6 flying body. With opponent at 6 life, extra lands are less valuable than a cheap threat that also draws cards.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Quantum Riddler as second discard (first was Into the Flood Maw)",
      "betterLine": "Discard a redundant land (Riverpyre Verge or Spirebluff Canal) instead of Quantum Riddler, keeping the Warp option as a cheap 4/6 flyer that draws a card on entry",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 125,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 1419
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Opponent is at 6 life with no board. Casting Stormchaser's Talent (U) creates a new untapped Otter token with prowess and pumps Duelist (via drawing with Frostcliff Siege Jeskai on combat damage). Casting Boomerang Basics on own permanent draws a card (increasing Duelist's power) and triggers prowess on Otters. By chaining spells pre-combat, Duelist of the Mind's power could reach 2-3+ with flying/vigilance, plus fresh Otter tokens attacking, potentially threatening lethal or near-lethal against an empty board at 6 life.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed without casting any spells before combat, leaving Duelist at low power and missing the chance to create additional attackers.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Stormchaser's Talent (U) to create a fresh untapped Otter token, then cast Boomerang Basics bouncing Stormchaser's Talent to draw a card and trigger prowess, maximizing Duelist's power and total combat damage against the opponent at 6 life with no blockers.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 134,
      "player": "Kimi25 Sarge",
      "decisionIndex": 1550
    }
  ],
